  "question": {
    "text": "Two ideal Carnot engines operate in cascade (all heat given up by one engine is used by the other engine to produce work) between temperatures, <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> . The temperature of the hot reservoir of the first engine is <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and the temperature of the cold reservoir of the second engine is <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> . <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> is temperature of the sink of first engine which is also the source for the second engine. How is <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math> related to <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> , if both the engines perform equal amount of work?"
  },
